-## Working with Stripe
+## Billing and Stripe
+
+Hosted plans are Free (1,000 published cards, 1 GB), Unlimited ($20 USD/month,
+5 GB), and Unlimited+ ($25 USD/month, 500 GB). Drafts do not count toward the
+free card limit; closed published cards do. Existing larger storage grants remain
+in effect; legacy exceptions never reduce a plan's allowance. Active and trialing subscriptions receive their plan's allowances;
+other statuses use Free unless the account is comped. Pending payment problems
+are resolved through the portal, not by purchasing a second subscription.
+
+Admins can upgrade from Account Settings. Checkout and customer management are
+hosted by Stripe. Signed subscription webhooks activate paid plans and apply portal changes;
+returning from Checkout alone does not grant paid allowances. Deleting a paid
+account first cancels its Stripe subscription; if Stripe is unavailable, the
+account remains open so deletion can be retried.
+
+Configure these existing deployment environment variables:
+
+| Variable | Purpose |
+| --- | --- |
+| `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY` | Stripe server-side API key |
+| `STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET` | Signing secret for this environment's webhook endpoint |
+| `STRIPE_MONTHLY_V1_PRICE_ID` | Unlimited: recurring monthly USD $20 price |
+| `STRIPE_MONTHLY_EXTRA_STORAGE_V1_PRICE_ID` | Unlimited+: recurring monthly USD $25 price |
+
+Create/verify the two recurring prices in the appropriate Stripe environment and
+configure its customer portal to allow switching between them and cancellation.
+Restrict the portal to those prices with a quantity of one. Register the
+**unprefixed** endpoint `https://<host>/stripe/webhooks` for
+`customer.subscription.created`, `customer.subscription.updated`, and
+`customer.subscription.deleted`. Unknown customers/events are acknowledged without
+changing accounts. Unknown prices never grant a paid plan. Replayed and older
+events are ignored. Scheduled cancellation keeps the plan until Stripe reports
+that the subscription has ended.
+
+Run the SaaS database migrations before deploying:
 
